@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@code{word-extract} finds a word wherever it appears in a set of LaTeX files
and copies the surrounding prose so you can read it in context.  It is the
companion of @code{index-extract} for the common case in which you did not tag
the passage with an @code{\index} key.  It shares that package's design, which
assumes one document file per day, the files organized into monthly subfolders,
and commands that choose files either by picking folders or by a date range read
from the file names.

The motivating use is to find every place you mentioned a word across a stretch
of days.  For example you can find every entry in September that mentions
@code{book}, so you can recover the book ideas you have not yet turned into
projects.

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

Put @file{word-extract.el} on your @code{load-path} and require it.

@example
(add-to-list 'load-path "/path/to/extract-word-el")
(require 'word-extract)

(setq word-extract-default-directory "~/2026words/Content/"
      word-extract-root-directory    "~/2026words/Content/"
      word-extract-default-days      7)
@end example

The repository ships an Info manual.  After @code{make info} you can install
@file{word-extract.info} into your Info tree, or read it in place.

@node Filename dates
@chapter Filename dates

The date commands read the date out of each file's name.  Two styles are
recognised out of the box.  An ISO date such as @code{2026-09-26} is matched by
@code{word-extract-date-regexp}.  A day-month-name-year date such as
@code{26September2026} is matched by @code{word-extract-date-named-regexp}.

Full month names and the common three-letter abbreviations both work, and the
month name is matched without regard to case.  If your file names follow some
other scheme, set @code{word-extract-date-named-regexp} so that its group one is
the day, group two the month name, and group three the year.  Failing that, set
@code{word-extract-date-regexp} to a numeric pattern whose groups are the year,
month, and day.

@node Mentions and context
@chapter Mentions and context

A mention is an occurrence of a word in a prose line.  By default a whole word
is matched, so @code{book} matches @code{book} and @code{Book} but not
@code{notebook} or @code{books}.  Set @code{word-extract-match-style} to
@code{substring} to match anywhere, or to @code{regexp} to treat the word as an
Emacs regexp.

A match that lands on a structural line, that is a line beginning with a LaTeX
command or a comment such as a heading, an @code{\index} entry, or a
@code{\begin}, is skipped, so the retrieved context stays prose.  See
@code{word-extract-boundary-regexp}.  The context is the paragraph containing the
mention, bounded by blank lines and structural lines.  Set
@code{word-extract-context-lines} to an integer to take that many lines on each
side instead.  When several mentions fall in one context, they collapse to a
single entry whose words are merged.

@node Commands
@chapter Commands

@table @kbd
@item M-x word-extract
Pick the files to scan, which may be several whole monthly subfolders and
individual files, then the word or words, and collect each mention with its
context into the results buffer.

@item M-x word-extract-last-days
Scan every dated file from the last @var{N} days, default seven.  Files are
found by searching your log root recursively and keeping those whose name holds
a date within range (@pxref{Filename dates}), so a week that spans two months is
handled for you.  With a prefix argument the result is written to an Org file
instead of the buffer.

@item M-x word-extract-range
Like @code{word-extract-last-days} but you give an explicit start and end date.
With a prefix argument the result goes to an Org file.

@item M-x word-extract-to-org
Pick files and words, then append the mentions to an Org file as one subtree.

@item M-x word-extract-again-words
Run again with the same files but new words.

@item M-x word-extract-again-files
Run again with the same words but a new file selection.
@end table

@node The results buffer
@chapter The results buffer

The mentions land in the @code{*word-extract*} buffer, each under a LaTeX
comment line that records the source file, the line number, the matched word or
words, and the section heading.  These key bindings are active there.

@table @kbd
@item C-c C-s
Save the buffer to a file.
@item C-c C-w
Copy just the context passages, without the label lines, to the kill ring.
@item C-c C-o
Write these same results to an Org file as a subtree.
@item C-c C-k
Run again with the same files but new words.
@item C-c C-f
Run again with the same words but a new file selection.
@end table

@node Org output
@chapter Org output

The Org commands append one subtree per run.  The top heading carries
@code{:WORDS:}, @code{:FILES:}, @code{:MENTIONS:}, and @code{:GENERATED:}
properties.  Each child heading is one mention and carries @code{:SOURCE:},
@code{:LINE:}, @code{:WORDS:}, and @code{:DATE:} properties, with the context
passage as its body.

@node Customization
@chapter Customization

@vtable @code
@item word-extract-default-directory
Directory where the file prompts start.

@item word-extract-root-directory
Root folder the date commands search recursively.

@item word-extract-default-days
Default number of days for @code{word-extract-last-days}.

@item word-extract-file-glob
Shell wildcard used when adding a whole directory.  The default is
@code{*.tex}.

@item word-extract-date-regexp
Regexp matching an ISO date in a file name.

@item word-extract-date-named-regexp
Regexp matching a day-month-name-year date in a file name.

@item word-extract-match-style
@code{word}, @code{substring}, or @code{regexp}.

@item word-extract-case-fold
If non-nil, word matching ignores case.

@item word-extract-include-heading
If non-nil, label each mention with its nearest preceding section heading.

@item word-extract-context-lines
Nil for the containing paragraph, or an integer for that many lines each side.

@item word-extract-boundary-regexp
Lines matching this regexp are structural and bound a context paragraph.

@item word-extract-heading-regexp
Regexp that matches a sectioning command.

@item word-extract-result-buffer
Name of the buffer that collects the mentions.

@item word-extract-result-major-mode
Major mode for the results buffer, turned on with its hooks suppressed.

@item word-extract-org-file
Default Org file for the Org-output commands.

@item word-extract-sort-files
If non-nil, scan files in sorted order, so dated names come out chronological.
@end vtable

@node Finding book ideas
@chapter Finding book ideas

This is the workflow the package was written for.  You suspect you described
several book ideas across the month but never tagged them.  You run @kbd{M-x
word-extract-range}, give the start and end of September, and the word
@code{book}.  The package searches the log root, keeps the dated files in range
even when they fall in two monthly subfolders, finds every prose mention of
@code{book}, skips the headings and @code{\index} entries, and collects each
mention with its paragraph of context in date order.  Press @kbd{C-c C-w} to
copy the passages, or run the command with a prefix argument to drop them into
an Org file where you can promote the promising ideas into projects.

